==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|fey|lang=enm}} "fated to die" from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|fæge|fǣge|lang=ang}} "doomed to die, timid" from {{proto|Germanic|faigijaz|cowardly, wicked|lang=en}} from {{proto|Indo-European|pAik-||pAig-|ill-meaning, bad|lang=en}}. Akin to {{osx}} {{term|fegi|fēgi|lang=osx}} ({{nl}} {{term|veeg|lang=nl}} "doomed, near death"), {{goh}} {{term|feigi|lang=goh}} "appointed for death, ungodly" ({{de}} {{term|feige|lang=de}} "cowardly"), {{non}} {{term|feigr|lang=non}} "doomed", {{ang}} {{term|fah|fāh|lang=ang}} "outlawed, hostile". More at {{l|en|foe}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/feɪ/}}
* {{audio|en-us-fey.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|eɪ}}
* Homophones: [[fay]]

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[magical|Magical]] or [[fairy]]like.
# [[strange|Strange]] or [[otherworldly]].
# [[spellbound|Spellbound]].
# {{dialectal|or|archaic}} About to die; [[fated]]; [[doomed]]; on the verge of sudden or violent death.
# {{obsolete}} [[dying|Dying]]; [[dead]].

